6:["$","section",null,{"className":"bg-indigo-deep border-t border-white/5","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 lg:px-10 py-24 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-10","children":[["$","$L2",null,{"className":"lg:col-span-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-brass-bright mb-4","children":"Competition History"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-washi text-balance","children":"Honour on the mat."}]]}],["$","$L2",null,{"delay":0.1,"className":"lg:col-span-8 space-y-5","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-body text-washi-dim leading-relaxed text-lg","children":"Our dojo started competing through karate tournaments. O-Sensei Reynolds was part of a pioneering group of Jiu-Jitsu black belts sent out to local karate tournaments to investigate how the sparring worked, and eventually to help develop the rules for what has since evolved into Sport Jiu-Jitsu."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"font-body text-washi-dim leading-relaxed text-lg","children":"Because of that early participation, Jiu-Jitsu fighters were accepted into open-style karate tournaments in kata, weapons, and kumite. O-Sensei's friendships with the senior karate instructors of the day paved the way for what we still do — many of the tournaments our students attend today are a continuation of friendships formed in the early 1970s."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"font-body text-washi-dim leading-relaxed text-lg","children":"Over the years, the Richmond Hill Budokan has produced World Champions, Martial Arts Hall of Fame recipients, and students who've gone on to movie and TV appearances — always in service of the same standard: honour in showing our craft, and honing our skills to keep improving."}]]}]]}]}]
